meta {
  name: 29 Transfer - Accept (RULE 1)
  type: http
  seq: 29
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/accession/transfers/{{transferId}}/accept
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{businessAccessToken}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "notes": "Accepted at receiving branch"
  }
}

tests {
  test("transition applied (or rejected by the state machine)", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.be.oneOf([200, 201, 422]);
  });
}

docs {
  Accept (§B.11.3) — **Received → Accepted**. **RULE 1:** for an INTERNAL transfer
  this clones the sample into the **receiving branch's In-House list** as a new
  `AccessionSample` at status `ACCEPTED` (fresh `ACC-` number, tests copied). All
  further processing happens on that In-House copy. An INTERNAL transfer with no
  destination assigned → 422 `TRANSFER_DESTINATION_MISSING`. Bulk:
  `POST /accession/transfers/bulk/accept`.

  Cancelled order: a single call on a transfer whose order was cancelled is refused
  (409 `TRANSFER_ORDER_CANCELLED`). In a bulk call with `skipInvalid: true` that transfer is
  SKIPPED instead (listed in `skipped` with `code: TRANSFER_ORDER_CANCELLED` and the reason);
  the other transfers are done. Bulk calls work through the ids in sorted order (so two
  overlapping bulk calls cannot deadlock); `succeeded` / `skipped` come back in the order
  the ids were sent.

  **Accept is refused when the receiving branch does not offer every test on the tube (Phase 1 safety net).**
  For a tube sent before the destination was checked: 422 `DESTINATION_LACKS_TEST`, "Your branch does not offer:
  <tests>, so this transfer cannot be accepted. Reject it, or send it back with Repeat." Nothing is created and the
  transfer stays Received; the receiving branch can Reject or Repeat it as usual. A bulk accept with `skipInvalid`
  skips that transfer with this code and reason. Tubes already accepted are untouched.

  **The receiving branch works the tube with ITS OWN copy of the test (Phase 2).** On Accept, inside the same
  transaction, the report and the receiving branch's copy of the tube are switched from the sender's Branch Master
  Data copy of the test to the receiver's: the report's `labTestId` (so the technician at the receiver is offered the
  receiver's parameters, methods, reference ranges and values), the copy's tests, sample definition and department,
  and `LabReport.performingBranchLabTestId` = the receiver's Lab Test List entry (its report template / PDF, TAT and
  processing window, department and signatory department apply while it holds the report). The receiver's entry is
  its default list's normal entry for the same tenant test (a "(Copy)" row and other lists come after). The order line
  (price, billing) is never changed. Nothing is refused for missing parameters: the technician screen says "No result
  parameters configured" and Save / Submit stay blocked without a value or a file. The TAT clock counts from the
  receiver's own accept. Tubes accepted before this change keep the sender's copy.

  **An OUTSOURCE transfer must be assigned before it moves.** A sample can be outsourced with no center and no
  logistics type (both optional, set later with Assign Center), but such a transfer can not be Picked Up / Received /
  Accepted: Pick Up needs a center AND a logistics type, Receive and Accept need the center. Otherwise 422
  `TRANSFER_OUTSOURCE_NOT_ASSIGNED`, "This outsource transfer has no center and no logistics type yet. Use Assign
  Center in In-House Orders to set it, then try again." (naming only what is missing; `details.missing` lists it).
  Nothing is changed. A bulk call with `skipInvalid` skips that transfer with this code and reason. This is the
  outsource counterpart of an INTERNAL transfer needing a destination branch and a logistics type; Forward (EXTERNAL)
  is not covered, and Repeat / Reject are not guarded. The free-text Update Status is not affected.
}
